 2. Meta takes fresh aim at businesses 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Meta is increasingly looking beyond consumers as it seeks to eventually turn its massive AI investment into a profitable business.

 Why it matters: The Facebook parent is moving into a market long dominated by companies such as Microsoft, bringing its consumer AI agent and broader AI infrastructure to businesses of all sizes.

 Driving the news: Meta today is announcing Muse for Small Business, which brings its consumer AI agent to a new market.

 Meta is adding connector support to a range of tools often used by small businesses, including Asana, Canva, Dropbox, Figma, Intuit QuickBooks, Lovable, Notion, Shopify, Slack, Stripe and Zoom. Separately, Meta yesterday announced Meta Enterprise Platform, a push aimed at selling its AI services to larger businesses. Zoom in: Meta is pitching Muse as a digital operations layer for small businesses that often lack the time or money to hire specialists for every function.

 For example, a business owner can ask Muse to analyze sales, campaigns and social activity and develop a growth plan. It can also help manage cash flow and inventory, draft customer communications and create ads and other content, including by analyzing what has worked previously. What they're saying: Meta has been testing Muse with businesses from before it launched publicly and about a third of early Muse users are already connecting it to some type of business account, Meta VP of AI products Vishal Shah told Axios.

 "People are using Muse to run their business," Shah said. "We're going to make it easier to do so." Tom Mulholland, owner of Mulholland Grocery in Malvern, Iowa, found out his store had been delisted from Google Maps because he missed an email. Muse helped him get reconnected. 
 
