 3. Florida asks court to halt ChatGPT development 
 
 By Josephine Walker and Avery Lotz 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 OpenAI CEO Sam Altman last week. Photo: John Lamparski/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Florida Attorney General James Uthmeier has asked for a temporary injunction against OpenAI and ChatGPT, claiming the company doesn't have the ability to properly regulate its own technology.

 Why it matters: The legal fight could further test how far states can go to restrict AI companies despite the federal government declining to enact guardrails.

 Florida's request specifically cites a Sept. 26 Axios scoop that OpenAI and Anthropic are facing tens of thousands of security incidents with their models, not the dozens that are publicly known. What they're saying: "Stop calling it safe," Uthmeier said in a video posted to X yesterday.

 "Stop pretending it's human. Stop selling it to kids. If Sam Altman meant what he said about slowing down, he can join our ask to the court." The other side: "People want to know AI is being developed safely, and that starts with what companies like ours do," said OpenAI spokesperson Drew Pusateri.

